erdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The document is titled 'Voyager 2 at Uranus' and the text states mission planners directed the spacecraft to Uranus.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-003\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a journey that would take about 4,5 years\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states the journey to Uranus would take about 4,5 years.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-004\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source states that the Jupiter encounter was optimized to ensure future planetary flybys would be possible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-005\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The Ur anus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source states the Uranus encounter's geometry was defined by possibility of future Saturn encounter.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-006\",\n      \"verdict\": \"PARTIAL\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source confirms Voyager 1 had 6.4 days of close study, but does not explicitly state this refers to Saturn.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-007\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 1's short-range observations\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source explicitly identifies Voyager 1, not Voyager 2, as the first human-made object to fly past Uranus.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-008\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source attributes observations to Voyager 1 on Jan. 31, 1987, not Voyager 2 on January 24, 1986.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-009\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-010\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-011\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source specifies the date as Jan. 24, 1968, not 1986 as claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-012\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states that Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-013\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source lists different moon name spellings: Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Cordelina, Bianca II.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-014\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a naming tradition begun in 1687\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source states naming tradition was begun in 1687, not 1787 as claimed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-015\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"three new rings in addition to the \\\"older\\\" eight rings\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states Voyager 2 discovered three new rings in addition to eight older rings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-016\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states Uranus has a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-017\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere are as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-018\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states evidence of boiling lake 479 miles (900 kilometers) below top cloud surface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-019\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source states rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-020\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source lists moons as Ariele and Umbrella, contradicting claim's spellings of Ariel and Umbriel.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-021\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source confirms Voyager 2 flew by Miranda at that range as its closest approach to any object.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-022\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Uranus itself appeared generally featureless\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-023\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source states Challenger killed six astronauts on Feb. 28, 1986, not seven on January 28, 1986.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms":110542,"type":"result","duration_ms":120788,"uuid":"80ff86db-af72-40ad-bf73-c6bf749ec5bb","ttft_stream_ms":717,"time_to_request_ms":111,"first_content_frame_ms":717,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
